Codebud NEWS                                                  -*- outline -*-

User-visible changes, newest first. Versions follow Semantic Versioning;
see "Versioning" in the manual's Development chapter.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (unreleased) [alpha]

The first release.  Codebud is a privacy-first, local coding agent: a
local Ollama model plans a request as steps, and codebud runs them under
a safety policy you can read and change.

** Using it

  codebud run, plan (and plan --execute) and chat; agent modes ask, auto,
  full-auto, plan and edit; sessions saved and resumed (chat --resume);
  codebud doctor, history, sessions, models, config, profiles, version.

  Project search: codebud index builds a per-project index, and the
  most relevant code goes into each plan as project context.

  Profiles bundle a mode, a privacy level, a policy and a prompt
  (built in: default, unattended; your own in ~/.config/codebud/profiles/).

  codebud skill install lets Claude Code or OpenClaw call codebud.

** Privacy

  Prompts go only to the Ollama on this machine by default; no cloud
  fallback, no telemetry.  A privacy level (locked, private, trusted)
  decides what may leave the machine; web search is off unless enabled
  and confirmed.  Stored data is owner-only, secrets in it are masked,
  and deleting it lists what goes and asks first.

  Optional encryption at rest with age: codebud key init, then
  encrypt = true; sessions, step history and project indexes.  Older
  plaintext can be converted with codebud key encrypt-existing.

** Safety

  Commands run only if on an allow-list, matched by whole words; arguments
  that run arbitrary code are refused, and catastrophic commands (power
  off, erase a disk, rm -r of / or a home directory) are refused whatever
  the allow-list says.  Command steps are isolated with bubblewrap: no
  network unless declared, project-only writes, secrets and codebud's own
  files hidden, an allow-listed environment.

  File steps write only inside the project, never into .git/ or
  .codebud/, and never read your secret files; every plan shows the real
  path, command or query of each step before it runs.

  A project's .codebud/ policy narrows your allow-list at once and widens
  it only once you trust the project (codebud trust); any change to it
  asks again.  A project's config.toml cannot redirect your prompts,
  weaken protection or set what costs you.

  An independent security review (three reviewers) found 3 critical,
  4 high, 5 medium and 14 low issues before this release; all are fixed
  or, where agreed, documented.

** Building and installing

  GNU Autotools (./bootstrap, ./configure, make, make check, make
  install) or pip; a Texinfo manual (info and HTML).  CI builds and
  tests on Ubuntu, macOS and Fedora.

** Known limitations

  Alpha: the command line, configuration and data formats may still
  change before 1.0.  Isolation needs bubblewrap and user namespaces
  (GNU/Linux); elsewhere steps run unisolated with a warning.  The
  security layer (codebud_sec: engagement file, evidence log) ships but
  is not used yet.
